          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:7242185c4ada477041fc336cddcdb2123954b4f418421fafca734d57afe6a73e:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_12c990119ac64b72804445dee75a521d",
      "statement": "solve_symbolic(relation, given, target, tolerance) solves relation == 0 for the target symbol by sympifying the relation, substituting sympified givens, and calling sp.solve inside try/except; it returns (value, note) where value is a float/int/str when numeric (via float(evalf()), optionally tolerance-rounded) or a simplified sympy expression when symbolic.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://solvers.py#L50-L90",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:639e3ddddf08de31d0af401f42eb58af459252ff32947464028a0091be7f25f7:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_64856b076b2346ffb5ff33302923b83d",
      "statement": "The physics magnitude convention [v1]: when a relation has multiple roots, solve_symbolic filters to real roots, selects the positive ones, takes the minimum positive value, falls back to the first real root, then to the first solution; ARCHITECTURE.md §8 frames this as applying to velocity-like targets (textbook problems expect the magnitude), and the benchmark's square-root problems (e.g. kin-13, kin-14) pass only because negative roots are discarded.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://ARCHITECTURE.md#L328-L331",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:42188fe2fdf405cab018870ff5cc283a1a9b86a84c1c703450f930bac19a3664:"
        },
